Looking at the code snippet of the `IOUtils.skip(InputStream, long)` method:
```java
 94 :     public static long skip(InputStream input, long numToSkip) throws IOException {
 95 :         long available = numToSkip;
 96 :         while (numToSkip > 0) {
 97 :             long skipped = input.skip(numToSkip);
 98 :             if (skipped == 0) {
 99 :                 break;
100 :             }
101 :             numToSkip -= skipped;
102 :         }
103 :         
104 :         return available - numToSkip;
105 :     }
```

The method iterates over the input stream, attempting to skip `numToSkip` bytes by repeatedly calling `input.skip(numToSkip)` until either all bytes have been skipped or the input stream reaches its end. 

The current bug report mentions that `IOUtils.skip()` does not adhere to its contract, specifically regarding skipping less than the requested number of bytes. The issue seems to be related to how the method handles the case when `skip` from the input stream returns 0.

